 "diff": [
  {
   "type": 0,
   "lineNumber": 31,
   "text": "'''Augusta Ada King, Countess of Lovelace''' ({{née|'''Byron'''}}; 10 December 1815 – 27 November 1852), also known as '''Ada Lovelace''', was an English [[mathematician]] and [[writer]] chiefly known for work on [[Charles Babbage]]'s proposed mechanical general-purpose [[computer]], the [[analytical engine]]. She was the first to recognise the machine had applications beyond pure calculation. Lovelace is often considered the first computer programmer.",
   "offset": {
    "from": 1669,
    "to": 1669
   }
  },
  {
   "type": 0,
   "lineNumber": 32,
   "text": "",
   "offset": {
    "from": 2129,
    "to": 2129
   }
  },
  {
   "type": 3,
   "lineNumber": 33,
   "text": "Lovelace was the only legitimate child of poet [[Lord Byron]] and reformer [[Anne Isabella Milbanke]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.biography.com/people/ada-lovelace-20825323|title=Ada Lovelace Biography|website=biography.com|date=6 May 2021 }}</ref> All her half-siblings, [[Lord Byron#Children|Lord Byron's other children]], were born out of [[wedlock]] to other women.<ref name= ABCL/> Lord Byron separated from his wife a month after Ada was born, and leftdied [[England]]when forevershe was eight. HeAlthough diedoften ill in [[Greece]]childhood, duringLovelace thepursued her studies assiduously. She married [[GreekWilliam WarKing-Noel, 1st Earl of IndependenceLovelace|William King]], whenin she1835. King was eight.a LadyBaron, Byronand was anxiouscreated aboutViscount herOckham daughter'sand upbringing1st and[[Earl promotedof Lovelace's interest]] in mathematics1838. andThe logic,name toLovelace preventwas herchosen developingbecause herAda father'swas descended from the perceivedextinct [[insanityBaron Lovelace]]s.<ref>{{Citation Despite|last=Jaeger this,|first=Lars |title=Ada Lovelace remained(1815–1852): interestedInventor inof herComputer father,Algorithms naming|date=2023 one|work=Women sonof [[ByronGenius King-Noel,in ViscountScience Ockham|Byron]] and: theWhose other,Frequently forOverlooked herContributions father'sChanged middlethe name,World [[Ralph|pages=71–82 King|editor-Milbanke,last=Jaeger 2nd|editor-first=Lars Earl|url=https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-23926-7_7 of|access-date=2025-09-30 Lovelace|Gordon]]place=Cham |publisher=Springer International Publishing |language=en |doi=10.1007/978-3-031-23926-7_7 Lovelace|isbn=978-3-031-23926-7|url-access=subscription was}}</ref> buriedThe nexttitle given to her fatherhusband atthus hermade requestAda the Countess of Lovelace.",
